{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" release, slice 2: the reader's page (design for council round 76)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status: revision 3, for council round 76c. No code yet.**\n     4\t\n     5\t**Revision 3 answers `codex-r76b.md`:**\n     6\t- the screen check compares every visible text of each checked card with a frozen list, in order and grouped by evidence, so an\n     7\t  extra line fails;\n     8\t- each dated card is frozen from its own dated package;\n     9\t- the timer stops when the list is visible;\n    10\t- the dark background is the literal `rgb(15, 20, 13)`.\n    11\t\n    12\tRevision 2 answered `codex-r76.md`:\n    13\t- source addresses are plain text, never links;\n    14\t- the escaping is written out as literal JSON escapes, with the charset and the CSP first in the file;\n    15\t- the format-1 checks are updated, and a lone page-hash error has its own test;\n    16\t- the screen check compares the expanded `v8` card with frozen expected entries;\n    17\t- the fetch mutant keeps its script running and must name `connect-src` and its destination;\n    18\t- the fallback, timer, phone-width and dark-scheme conditions are made observable;\n    19\t- the text filter is dropped. Slice 1 is built and accepted (D759: `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11,\n    20\t`RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`). Council 73 (D758) ordered the exporter first and the reader's screen second. This slice completes D756's\n    21\tscreen check: a $0 check of the reader's screen on a stored number question that has a forecast and history, where only the\n    22\tquestion, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 0. Scope\n    25\t\n    26\t- **Built:**\n    27\t  - a page that is a pure function of the package data;\n    28\t  - the package gains that page (format 2);\n    29\t  - the package check proves the page equals that function of the data;\n    30\t  - a headless-browser check of the `old-1/v8` screen.\n    31\t- **The page reads only the package's data,** embedded in it.\n    32\t  - No network request of any kind: no live POC service, no `/library` fetch, no translated or prepared JSON, no composite trials,\n    33\t    no fonts or images from outside.\n    34\t  - No idea submission control or route (council 74).\n    35\t- **Not in scope:**\n    36\t  - the POC's page, API and MCP, unchanged;\n    37\t  - colleagues' page;\n    38\t  - the idea path;\n    39\t  - any model call;\n    40\t  - commit, publish, deploy (Alex's).\n    41\t\n    42\t**Why a separate page, not the POC's library UI.** The POC's components read the `thesis-ui/2` contract, which needs a ladder,\n    43\tscores and judgements for every thesis; a number question in the package has none. Adapting those shared components would change\n    44\tthe POC, which stays unchanged (design §2, D757). A small page written for the package has nothing to adapt and nothing that can\n    45\treach the network.\n    46\t\n    47\t## 1. What is built\n    48\t\n    49\t**A visible fallback.** The page starts with the line \"This release page needs JavaScript to show its questions.\" The script removes it\n    50\tonce the list is built. A browser that blocks the inline script keeps that line (`<noscript>` would stay hidden there; council\n    51\t76).\n    52\t\n    53\t**In `src/hivemind/release.py`:**\n    54\t- **`FORMAT = 'hivemind-release/2'`.** The data's allow-list (`release-allow-list/1`) is unchanged.\n    55\t- **`PACKAGE_FILES = {'manifest.json', 'release-data.json', 'index.html'}`.** The manifest's closed set gains `page_sha256`.\n    56\t- **`render_page(data_bytes) -> bytes`:** one fixed template, the module constant `PAGE_TEMPLATE`, with the data inserted once.\n    57\t  - **The data** goes into `<script type=\"application/json\" id=\"release-data\">`, written from the exact `release-data.json` bytes,\n    58\t    with each of these characters replaced by its six-character JSON escape: `<` by `\\u003c`, `>` by `\\u003e`, `&` by `\\u0026`,\n    59\t    U+2028 by `\\u2028`, U+2029 by `\\u2029`. These replacements stay valid JSON and parse back to the same data. The block can then\n    60\t    hold no `</script` and no `<!--`.\n    61\t  - **The file starts** with `<!doctype html>`, then `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then the CSP `<meta>`, before any style, script or\n    62\t    data.\n    63\t  - **A Content-Security-Policy `<meta>`:**\n    64\t    `default-src 'none'; script-src 'sha256-<inline script>'; style-src 'sha256-<inline style>'; img-src 'none'; font-src 'none';\n    65\t    connect-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'`. The two hashes are computed from the template's own script and style,\n    66\t    so the browser refuses any other script, style or connection.\n    67\t  - **The script** builds the page only with `document.createElement`, `textContent` and `className`. It creates no `<a>` element,\n    68\t    no `href` and no `src`. It never uses `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write`, `eval`, `Function`, or any network API\n    69\t    (`fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket`, `EventSource`, `navigator.sendBeacon`, `import()`, `Worker`).\n    70\t  - **The style** uses system fonts and inline colour tokens copied from `web/design-system/tokens.css`. It has a dark scheme under\n    71\t    `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, and it works at phone width.\n    72\t- **`write_package`** also writes `index.html = render_page(data bytes)` and records its sha256.\n    73\t- **`check_package`** also:\n    74\t  - reads `index.html` once through the same folder handle;\n    75\t  - requires its bytes to equal `render_page(the data bytes it read)` exactly;\n    76\t  - requires the manifest's `page_sha256` to match.\n    77\t\n    78\t  A page that differs by one byte from the function of the data is refused. So a page cannot carry anything the data does not hold,\n    79\t  and no code the template does not hold.\n    80\t\n    81\t**What the page shows,** in package order. Each shown value sits in an element with a fixed class, so the screen check can read it:\n    82\t`question-text`, `question-status`, `question-forecast`, `question-answer`, `evidence-count`, `evidence-summary`, `evidence-quote`,\n    83\t`evidence-meta`, `source-title`, `source-publisher`, `source-day`, `source-address`.\n    84\t- **A header:** \"Hivemind release\", the package's day, and the count of questions by class.\n    85\t- **Each question:**\n    86\t  - its question text and \"Open\" or \"Closed\";\n    87\t  - for a number question, the marker \"Forecast unavailable\";\n    88\t  - for an event or Yes/No question, \"Answer: <label>\", or \"Not yet answered\" when the answer is null;\n    89\t  - \"<n> pieces of evidence\", and a `<details>` element. When opened, it builds the evidence list:\n    90\t    - each piece shows its summary, the quote, and a line with tier, speaker, and original speaker and day;\n    91\t    - each source shows title, publisher, day and its address as plain text. The page has no links, so neither a click nor a script\n    92\t      can make a request (council 76).\n    93\t\n    94\t  Evidence is built only when opened, so the first screen stays fast on a 6 MB package.\n    95\t\n    96\t## 2. State × event (the page and the check)\n    97\t\n    98\t| State / event | What happens |\n    99\t|---|---|\n   100\t| Package with no questions | \"No questions as of <day>\" |\n   101\t| A question with no evidence | \"No evidence in force\", and no `<details>` |\n   102\t| A source address of any scheme, `javascript:` included | shown as text; the page has no links |\n   103\t| Text holding `</script>`, `<img onerror=…>`, `&` or U+2028 | the embed escapes it, the page shows it as text, and the JSON parses back identical |\n   104\t| The page is opened from disk, a static host or any path | it reads only its embedded data; nothing else exists to read |\n   105\t| The browser blocks the inline script | the fallback line stays visible; nothing else renders, and the page holds no forecast to leak |\n   106\t| A page edited after the build (a fetch added, a field added) | `check_package` refuses: the bytes differ from `render_page(data)` |\n   107\t| The template changes in a later version | old packages fail `check_package` under the new code by design; a new template is a new format version |\n   108\t| A dark scheme and a phone width | checked in §6: at 390 px wide the page has no horizontal scroll; with the dark scheme emulated, the body's background is the dark token |\n   109\t\n   110\t## 3. Value × reader\n   111\t\n   112\t- Every value the page shows comes from the embedded data; the page reads no other value.\n   113\t- The page's readers are a person's browser and the browser check.\n   114\t- The data's readers are unchanged from slice 1: `check_package`, `check_origins`, the tests. The page adds one reader of the data,\n   115\t  the page script, and no new writer.\n   116\t\n   117\t## 4. New path × old checks\n   118\t\n   119\t| Slice-1 check | Slice 2 |\n   120\t|---|---|\n   121\t| Closed set of the data, origin check, staged check before the no-replace publication, the no-replace report | unchanged |\n   122\t| The manifest's closed set | gains `page_sha256`; `format` becomes `hivemind-release/2` |\n   123\t| Exactly the package files, each read once, no links, no pipes | the same rule over three files |\n   124\t| The literal-values rule of verification (council 75d) | applied to the page checks as well |\n   125\t| `release_check.py` pins `hivemind-release/1`; its helpers that forge data refresh only the data hash | the pin becomes `/2`. The forging helpers also regenerate `index.html = render_page(forged data)` and its hash, so a forged forecast still reaches the store comparison and is refused there, with its exact reason |\n   126\t\n   127\t## 5. Tests (`tests/test_release.py`) and mutants\n   128\t\n   129\t1. **Determinism.** `render_page` of the fixture data twice gives the same bytes.\n   130\t2. **Escaping.**\n   131\t   - A fixture quote holding `</script><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`, an `&` and a U+2028 is embedded with the literal escapes.\n   132\t   - The page bytes hold no `</script` inside the data block and no `<!--`.\n   133\t   - Parsing the block back gives the data unchanged.\n   134\t   - In the browser check (§6), on the fixture page with that evidence expanded, the exact quote is visible as text, and the page has\n   135\t     no `img` element.\n   136\t3. **No network code.**\n   137\t   - The template's script contains none of the listed APIs, and no `createElement('a')`, `href` or `src`.\n   138\t   - Its CSP is exactly the policy above, with the hashes of its own script and style.\n   139\t4. **The package check:**\n   140\t   - a page changed by one byte, with the manifest hash refreshed, is refused with its exact reason;\n   141\t   - a page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added, its CSP hash and manifest hash both updated, is refused, because it\n   142\t     is not `render_page(data)`;\n   143\t   - **a correct page with only `page_sha256` wrong is refused with its exact reason**, which catches a deleted hash check;\n   144\t   - a missing page and a fourth file are each refused;\n   145\t   - all three files are read once through the folder handle, and the page bytes compared are the bytes hashed.\n   146\t5. **Literal identifiers:** `hivemind-release/2` and `release-allow-list/1`, in the tests and the acceptance script.\n   147\t\n   148\t**Mutants:**\n   149\t- `render_page` not escaping `<`;\n   150\t- the script using `innerHTML` for the quote;\n   151\t- a source address rendered as a link;\n   152\t- `check_package` not comparing the page with `render_page`;\n   153\t- `check_package` not checking `page_sha256`;\n   154\t- the CSP hash computed over a different script.\n   155\t\n   156\t## 6. The $0 screen check (`release_screen.mjs`, run once)\n   157\t\n   158\t- **Setup.** Headless Chrome from `/Applications/Google Chrome.app`, driven through its DevTools protocol with Node's built-in\n   159\t  WebSocket, on a fresh profile in the scratchpad. Each page is opened from `file://`.\n   160\t- **Packages:** `old-1` at its default day, and the two dated leak packages (`old-3` at 2026-08-25, `old-1` at 2026-08-28), built by\n   161\t  the slice-1 command under format 2.\n   162\t\n   163\t**Recorded for each page:**\n   164\t- the page's own network requests (`Network.requestWillBeSent` for the page target). Only the document itself may appear;\n   165\t- console errors, exceptions and CSP violations (`Runtime.exceptionThrown`, `Log.entryAdded`). Zero are allowed.\n   166\t\n   167\t**Observation.** The `Network`, `Log` and `Runtime` domains are enabled before navigation and stay enabled through every expansion.\n   168\tAny of these fails the page:\n   169\t- a request other than the document;\n   170\t- a `Log.entryAdded` error;\n   171\t- a `Runtime.exceptionThrown`;\n   172\t- a `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` of type `error`.\n   173\t\n   174\t**Expected values, frozen before the run.** `release_screen.mjs` reads `screen-expected.json`. It is written before the first screen\n   175\trun, with its sha256 recorded in the result, from the accepted slice-1 packages:\n   176\t- `poc/data/release-check-2026-11-23g/old-1/package` for `v8`;\n   177\t- `old-3/package-2026-08-25` for `old-3/v36` (at that day it asks whether NVIDIA will begin shipping Rubin Ultra);\n   178\t- `old-1/package-2026-08-28` for `old-1/v10`.\n   179\t\n   180\tFor each checked card it holds the card's complete visible text, as a list in document order:\n   181\t- **collapsed:** question, status, the marker or the answer, the evidence count;\n   182\t- **expanded:** the same, then for each evidence entry in order its own group: summary, quote, meta line, and per source its title,\n   183\t  publisher, day and address.\n   184\t\n   185\tThe page's day is held for each package.\n   186\t\n   187\t**How a card is read.** The check walks the card's text nodes in document order. It keeps each text whose element is rendered (a\n   188\tnon-zero box, not `display: none` or `visibility: hidden`), and groups them by the enclosing evidence item. The read list must\n   189\tequal the frozen list exactly. An extra visible line anywhere in the card fails: for example, \"Forecast: $361 billion\", Morningstar's\n   190\tgenuine figure shown as if it were the system's. So does a missing, duplicated, regrouped or reordered entry.\n   191\t\n   192\t**The screen of `old-1/v8`:**\n   193\t- **Collapsed:** the card's read list equals the frozen collapsed list: \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for\n   194\t  FY2027?\", \"Open\", \"Forecast unavailable\", \"1284 pieces of evidence\".\n   195\t- **Expanded:** the read list equals the frozen expanded list of 1,284 groups. The full Morningstar quote and its attribution are\n   196\t  included, and \"Bullish\" appears only where a frozen source text holds it.\n   197\t- **Closed and reopened once:** the read list is the same.\n   198\t- **No link anywhere on the page:** zero `a` elements, zero `[href]`, zero `[src]`.\n   199\t- **A screenshot** of the expanded card's top is saved with the result.\n   200\t\n   201\t**The leak screens.** For `old-3/v36` at 2026-08-25 and `old-1/v10` at 2026-08-28:\n   202\t- the page's day equals its package's;\n   203\t- the card's read list, collapsed and expanded, equals the frozen list from that dated package: its question, status, \"Answer: Yes\",\n   204\t  \"1 piece of evidence\", then its single evidence entry and source;\n   205\t- so the entry is visible, and nothing else is: neither judgement's explanation, nor \"around $1 trillion\", \"rung 4\" or \"at rung\".\n   206\t\n   207\t**The decisive negative** (a check of the check). A copy of the `old-1` page whose script adds one visible line, \"Forecast: $361\n   208\tbillion\", to the `v8` card, with its CSP hash recomputed, must fail the `v8` screen comparison. Every named element stays intact.\n   209\t\n   210\t**The escaping screen.** The fixture page of test 2, with that evidence expanded, shows the exact quote as text and has no `img`\n   211\telement.\n   212\t\n   213\t**The fetch mutant.**\n   214\t- **Setup:** a copy of the `old-1` page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added to its script, and the CSP script hash\n   215\t  recomputed for the altered script, so the script runs.\n   216\t- **It must fail the check for the right reason:**\n   217\t  - the normal list renders;\n   218\t  - a `Log.entryAdded` security error names `http://127.0.0.1:59999/library` and `connect-src`.\n   219\t\n   220\t  A page whose script is blocked wholesale does not earn the credit.\n   221\t- **Measured on this Mac (Chrome 158, headless, scratchpad probe):** a CSP-blocked fetch produces no `Network.requestWillBeSent`. It\n   222\t  produces a `security` error naming the address and the directive, and a `javascript` error.\n   223\t- **The probe page named port 8788.** Chrome refused it under the CSP before connecting (\"Refused to connect\"), so no request reached\n   224\t  that service. Port 59999 is not on the fetch specification's blocked-port list, and nothing listens there.\n   225\t\n   226\t**Phone width and dark scheme.**\n   227\t- With `Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride` at 390 × 844, `document.scrollingElement.scrollWidth` is at most 390.\n   228\t- With `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, the body's computed background is exactly `rgb(15, 20, 13)` (the\n   229\t  shared `--ink`, used as the dark page colour). In light, it is `rgb(245, 242, 235)` (`--paper`).\n   230\t\n   231\t**Budget,** set before the first control:\n   232\t- from `Page.navigate` until the full question list is visible (every question card rendered with a non-zero box, and the fallback\n   233\t  line gone), at most 3 s for the `old-1` page in headless Chrome;\n   234\t- the page's size at most the data's bytes plus 64 KB.\n   235\t\n   236\t## 7. Prior decisions\n   237\t\n   238\tD726, D756, D757, D758, D759; council 74 (no idea input); council 75d (literal expected values); D690 (poc-v118 stays the product);\n   239\tD163 (English product text).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r76b.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round76-brief.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r76b.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round76-brief.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**Not ready to build.** A `v8` card could show both “Forecast unavailable” and “Forecast: $361 billion” and pass the proposed screen check. The $361 billion is a genuine Morningstar estimate. Showing it separately as Hivemind’s forecast would change what the reader sees while leaving every checked field correct. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:174](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:174) · [RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:124](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md:124)\n     2\t\n     3\t| Round-76 finding | Revision-2 assessment |\n     4\t|---|---|\n     5\t| 1. Source links | **Closed.** Addresses render as plain text. The expanded-page check requires zero links, `href` attributes, or `src` attributes. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:82](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:82) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:179](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:179) |\n     6\t| 2. Embedded JSON | **Closed.** The literal escapes and header order are explicit. The browser must show the fixture’s exact quote after expansion and find zero image elements. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:48](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:48) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:189](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:189) |\n     7\t| 3. Format 2 and package checks | **Closed in design.** The checker hashes and compares the same page bytes, and a wrong page hash alone must fail. The instruction to regenerate the page for altered test data applies to both existing helpers, including the one in the test suite. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:114](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:114) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:130) · [test_release.py:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_release.py:180) |\n     8\t| 4. What the cards actually show | **Still open.** Revision 2 pins all 1,284 `v8` entries, preserves genuine uses of “Bullish,” and checks reopening. Two gaps remain below. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:165](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:165) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:173](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:173) |\n     9\t| 5. Blocked fetch | **Closed.** Observation starts before navigation. The altered script must render the normal list, and Chrome must name both the intended address and `connect-src` in its security error. I found no further request path in the specified page. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:158](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:158) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:192](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:192) |\n    10\t| 6. Browser acceptance conditions | **Partly closed.** The fallback, navigation start time, and 390-pixel width check are concrete. The timer still stops when the list exists in the DOM, which can precede visibility. The dark-background check also lacks a fixed expected colour. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:40](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:40) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:205](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:205) · [tokens.css:3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/web/design-system/tokens.css:3) |\n    11\t\n    12\t**Two corrections are needed before build.**\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **Check the whole visible card.** Reading named elements and searching the remaining text for five labels, five conditions, and one forecast prefix lets the extra “Forecast: $361 billion” line pass. Compare the complete visible `v8` card with its expected contents, collapsed and expanded, preserving the grouping of each quote with its sources. A decisive test is to add that one visible line while leaving every named element intact. Use the same visibility requirement for the timer’s end point so a hidden list cannot meet the three-second budget. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:176](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:176) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:180) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:210](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:210)\n    15\t2. **Take each dated card’s expected contents from its own accepted package.** Line 166 names only the default September 20 `old-1` package. I checked the stored files. Its `v36` asks about MediaTek financing. The August 25 `old-3/v36` asks whether NVIDIA will begin shipping Rubin Ultra. Each dated card has exactly one evidence entry, yet both proposed checks could pass with an empty expanded panel because they ask only whether forbidden phrases are absent. Freeze each day, question, answer, and single evidence entry from its dated package, then require that entry and its source to be visible. The existing acceptance script identifies both package paths. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:165](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:165) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:184](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:184) · [release_check.py:208](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:208)\n    16\t\n    17\t**Optional.** Choose the literal dark background before the first run. The referenced stylesheet defines `--ink` as `#0f140d` and declares a light colour scheme. If `--ink` is the intended dark background, the browser check can expect `rgb(15, 20, 13)` directly. [tokens.css:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/web/design-system/tokens.css:8) · [tokens.css:21](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/web/design-system/tokens.css:21)\n    18\t\n    19\tThis was a read-only design review against the stored packages. Slice 2 has no implementation yet, so browser behavior remains to be verified during the build. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:3)     1\t# Council round 76: review the slice-2 design (the reader's page) before any code (read-only)\n     2\t\n     3\tYou are an independent reviewer. Do not edit any file. The repository is /Users/lchingen/hivemind. Cite file:line.\n     4\t\n     5\t## What to review\n     6\t\n     7\t`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md`, the design for slice 2 of the \"Forecast unavailable\" release. Slice 1 is\n     8\tbuilt and accepted (D759 at the end of `docs/DECISIONS-log.md`; `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11; `RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`;\n     9\t`src/hivemind/release.py`, `scripts/release_build.py`, `tests/test_release.py`). D756 asks for a $0 check of the reader's screen on\n    10\ta stored number question with a forecast and history: only the question, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear. D758\n    11\tordered the exporter first, the screen second. Council 74 deferred idea submission.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Questions\n    14\t\n    15\t1. **Leaks.** Can anything other than the package data reach the reader's screen, or any network request leave the page? Consider:\n    16\t   the CSP, inline script and style hashes, `file://` origin rules, links, `<details>`, `noscript`, the escaping of the embedded JSON.\n    17\t2. **The page = `render_page(data)` rule.** Is that the right structural check for the page? Is it sound with a read-once package? Is\n    18\t   the format bump to `hivemind-release/2` the right handling?\n    19\t3. **The screen check (§6).** Will CDP on headless Chrome show a CSP-blocked fetch as a request or a violation? Is the v8 screen\n    20\t   assertion set right, and literal enough (council 75d's rule)? Is the budget set before the control?\n    21\t4. **The tables (§2–§4) and tests (§5).** Is any cell blank or wrong? Is any test unable to fail without its code?\n    22\t5. **Scope.** Is anything in this design scope drift beyond D756/D758, or a change to the POC?\n    23\t6. **Verdict:** ready to build, or not ready with each serious finding.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Out of scope:**\n    26\t- the frozen judging, opening and extraction pieces;\n    27\t- the closed v13s line;\n    28\t- slice 1's accepted internals, unless slice 2 breaks them;\n    29\t- the idea path;\n    30\t- the recorded evidence-summary disagreement;\n    31\t- wording.\n    32\t\n    33\tReport only findings that change what is built or whether it is safe.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
